@database NilTerm.guide
@author "NilBBS"
@node Main "NilTerm"

@{b}NilTerm@{ub} - an ANSI BBS terminal for the Amiga

NilTerm calls BBSes the way SyncTERM does on a PC: its own screen, 80 columns
of the IBM PC font in the 16 PC colours, a dialing directory, auto-login, and
ZMODEM downloads that start by themselves.  It talks telnet, rlogin and raw
TCP, and dials out with a modem.  It is also the sysop terminal that comes
with NilBBS.

  @{" Needs            " link Needs}  what NilTerm runs on
  @{" Starting         " link Starting}  icon, Tool Types and Shell arguments
  @{" Dialing directory" link Directory}  your list of systems
  @{" A system's entry " link Entry}  connection, login, screen, speed
  @{" Auto-login       " link Login}  your name and password, sent for you
  @{" Modems           " link Modem}  dialling out on the serial port
  @{" While on a call  " link Keys}  the keys
  @{" File transfers   " link Transfers}  ZMODEM, YMODEM, XMODEM
  @{" Settings         " link Settings}  folders, ZMODEM, the modem
  @{" Scrollback & logs" link Scrollback}  looking back, capturing, the clipboard
  @{" Files it keeps   " link Files}  where things are saved
  @{" Credits          " link Credits}  the font, the licences
@endnode

@node Needs "What NilTerm needs"
@{b}What NilTerm needs@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 3.0 or newer, a 68020 or better.
  - For telnet / rlogin / raw: a TCP/IP stack with bsdsocket.library -
    Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami, or an emulator's bsdsocket emulation.
    For a modem: just the serial port (serial.device or a card's driver).
  - A 640x400 16-colour screen mode: a graphics card's 640x400 or 640x480 mode,
    or the chipset's High Res Laced.  With no 400-line mode it falls back to
    640x200 with a squashed font.
  - asl.library (file requesters) and iffparse.library (clipboard) - both come
    with the OS.  Without them only those features are missing.
@endnode

@node Starting "Starting NilTerm"
@{b}Starting NilTerm@{ub}

Double-click the icon: the @{"dialing directory" link Directory} opens.

From a Shell:
@{fg shine}  NilTerm [PORT=]n [HOST=name] [MODEID=hex] [NATIVE] [SMALL]@{fg text}

  HOST=, PORT=  call this system (telnet) straight away, no directory;
                NilTerm quits when the call ends.  PORT= alone calls this
                Amiga (127.0.0.1).
  MODEID=hex    use this screen mode (a display ID, as the ScreenMode prefs
                show it).
  NATIVE        don't pick a graphics card mode: use the chipset.
  SMALL         640x200 with half-height letters.
  WATCH=n       (NilBBS only) watch node n's caller's screen, read-only.

The same words work as Tool Types on the icon, e.g. HOST=bbs.example.org and
PORT=23 to make an icon that calls one system.
@endnode

@node Directory "The dialing directory"
@{b}The dialing directory@{ub}

Your list of systems, with how many times you've called each and when.

  Up/Down, PgUp/PgDn   pick
  Return               call the one picked
  A                    add a system (@{"the entry form" link Entry})
  E                    edit the one picked
  D, Del               delete it (asks first)
  C                    quick connect: type host:port (port 23 if left out);
                       the system is added to the list
  S                    @{"Settings" link Settings}
  Help                 the keys and credits
  Q, Esc               quit

When a call ends you're back in the directory.
@endnode

@node Entry "A system's entry"
@{b}A system's entry@{ub}  (A or E in the directory)

One line per setting.  Up/Down picks a line, Return changes it (type, then
Return), Left/Right steps through the choices on a < choice > line, Del
clears a text.  S saves; Esc leaves (it asks whether to keep any changes).

  Name              what the directory calls it
  Connection        telnet - most BBSes
                    raw    - plain TCP, no telnet talk
                    rlogin - port 513; logs you in with the name (and, as
                             Synchronet expects, the password)
                    modem  - dial out on the serial port (@{"Modems" link Modem})
  Address / number  host name or address; for a modem, the number to dial
                    (blank = a direct serial line: null-modem cable, or a
                    WiFi modem in transparent mode)
  Port              telnet 23, rlogin 513; 0 = the NilBBS on this Amiga,
                    whatever port it listens on
  User name,
  Password          your login on that system (@{"Auto-login" link Login})
  Auto-login        on: answer the name and password prompts by itself;
                    off: Alt+L sends them when you want
  Screen            PC: the IBM font and character set (most BBSes).
                    Amiga: the Topaz font from ROM, the Amiga (Latin-1)
                    character set, and the Amiga's one-byte CSI (0x9B) - for
                    BBSes that send Amiga ANSI.
  Line speed        full speed, or show the text at a modem's pace (300 to
                    115200 bps) - ANSI animations were drawn for that.
                    Alt+Up / Alt+Down change it during a call.
  iCE colours       when the BBS asks (ESC[?33h), or always: the "blink"
                    attribute gives a bright background instead of blinking
                    text, as iCE art expects
  Download folder   this system's downloads go here; blank = the Settings one
@endnode

@node Login "Auto-login"
@{b}Auto-login@{ub}

Put your user name and password in the system's @{"entry" link Entry}.  With
Auto-login on, NilTerm watches the first two minutes of a call: when the BBS
asks for your name (a prompt with name, handle, login, user or alias in it,
ending in : or ?) it types the name; when it asks for a password it types the
password.  Each is sent once.

Alt+L sends them by hand: the name then the password, or just the password if
the name has gone already.

@{b}The password is kept in ENVARC:NilTerm.lst as plain text@{ub} (as SyncTERM keeps
its own).  Anyone who can read your disk can read it - leave it blank for a
system that matters, and type it yourself.
@endnode

@node Modem "Modems"
@{b}Modems@{ub}

Set the port up once in @{"Settings" link Settings}: the device (serial.device, or your
serial card's driver), its unit, the speed between the Amiga and the modem,
hardware handshake (RTS/CTS - leave it on for a modem), the init string (ATZ)
and the dial command (ATDT, or ATDP for pulse).

Then make an entry with Connection "modem" and the phone number.  Calling it
sends the init string, dials, and shows what the modem says; CONNECT starts the
call, BUSY / NO CARRIER / NO ANSWER end it.  Esc stops the dialling.

Alt+H (or Alt+D) hangs up: +++, ATH0, and DTR drops as the port closes.  A
call also ends when the modem says NO CARRIER.

With no number the entry is a @{b}direct line@{ub}: nothing is dialled, you're on as
soon as the port opens.  A null-modem cable without handshake wires needs
Hardware handshake off; otherwise nothing is sent, and NilTerm says so after
ten seconds.

Auto-login, the Amiga screen, the line speed and file transfers all work over
a modem as over telnet.
@endnode

@node Keys "While on a call"
@{b}While on a call@{ub}

The bottom line is the status bar: the system, how long you've been on, CAP
while a capture runs, the emulated line speed, and the Alt keys.  The BBS gets
an 80x24 screen above it, as SyncTERM gives it.

  Alt+D       back to the dialing directory (hangs up)
  Alt+H       hang up
  Alt+L       send your @{"user name and password" link Login}
  Alt+B       @{"scrollback" link Scrollback}
  Alt+C       @{"capture" link Scrollback} the session to a file, on / off
  Alt+U       @{"upload" link Transfers} a file
  Alt+R       @{"receive" link Transfers} a file (ZMODEM, YMODEM, XMODEM)
  Alt+Up      line speed faster (past 115200: full speed)
  Alt+Down    line speed slower (from full speed: 115200)
  Alt+S       @{"Settings" link Settings}
  Alt+X       quit NilTerm (so does Amiga+Q)
  Amiga+C     copy the screen to the clipboard
  Amiga+V     paste the clipboard (as if typed)
  Help        the keys and credits

The cursor keys send what PC terminals send; Shift+Up/Down are PgUp/PgDn,
Shift+Left/Right are Home/End, and a PC keyboard's Home/End/PgUp/PgDn/Insert
keys work too.  Accented letters are sent in the PC character set (in the
Amiga character set on an Amiga-screen entry).

(NilTerm 1.1 had capture on Alt+L; it is Alt+C now, as in SyncTERM.)
@endnode

@node Transfers "File transfers"
@{b}File transfers@{ub}

@{b}Downloads.@{ub}  Pick ZMODEM on the BBS and just wait: NilTerm spots the
transfer starting and receives it.  The status bar shows the file and how much
has arrived; Esc cancels.  For YMODEM or XMODEM, start the download on the
BBS, then press Alt+R and pick the protocol (XMODEM asks for a file name, as
it doesn't carry one).

@{b}Uploads.@{ub}  Start the upload on the BBS, press Alt+U, pick the protocol
and the file.

@{b}Where downloads go:@{ub} the system's own download folder if its directory
entry has one, else the download folder in @{"Settings" link Settings}.  The very
first time, if neither is set, NilTerm asks and remembers the answer.

Transfers run at full speed whatever the emulated line speed is.
@endnode

@node Settings "Settings"
@{b}Settings@{ub}  (S in the directory, Alt+S during a call)

  Download folder     where downloads are saved (a directory entry's own
                      folder wins)
  Upload folder       where the upload file requester opens
  Capture folder      where Alt+C log files go
  ZMODEM downloads    "start by themselves", or "Alt+R only"
  Serial device       for @{"modem" link Modem} entries: serial.device, or a card's
  Serial unit         driver and its unit
  Serial speed        Amiga <-> modem, bps
  Hardware handshake  RTS/CTS on or off
  Modem init          sent before dialling (ATZ); blank = nothing
  Dial command        put before the number (ATDT)

Up/Down picks, Return changes (a folder requester, typing, or the next
choice), Left/Right step through choices, Esc goes back and saves.
@endnode

@node Scrollback "Scrollback, capture, the clipboard"
@{b}Scrollback@{ub}  (Alt+B)
The last 2000 lines that scrolled off the top.  Up/Down, PgUp/PgDn (or
Shift+Up/Down), Home/End; any other key goes back to the call.

@{b}Capture@{ub}  (Alt+C)
Logs everything the BBS sends from now on.  "Raw ANSI" keeps the colours and
cursor moves (view it later in NilTerm or SyncTERM); "Plain text" keeps only
the words.  An existing file grows.  Alt+C again stops.

@{b}Clipboard@{ub}
Amiga+C copies what's on screen as text (the PC's line-drawing characters
become + - | #).  Amiga+V types the clipboard into the call.
@endnode

@node Files "Files NilTerm keeps"
@{b}Files NilTerm keeps@{ub}

  ENVARC:NilTerm.lst     the dialing directory (plain text: [name], then
                         host=, port=, type=, user=, password=, ... -
                         passwords are NOT encrypted)
  ENVARC:NilTerm.prefs   the settings

Both are made the first time they're needed; delete them to start afresh.
@endnode

@node Credits "Credits"
@{b}Credits@{ub}

NilTerm is part of NilBBS, a telnet BBS for AmigaOS.  Its file transfers use
NilBBS's own ZMODEM and X/YMODEM code.  NilTerm is released under the MIT
licence - see the LICENSE file.

The screen font is IBM VGA 8x16 from "The Ultimate Oldschool PC Font Pack" by
VileR, https://int10h.org/oldschool-pc-fonts/ - licensed under Creative
Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0),
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
In the Amiga screen mode the font is the Topaz in the Kickstart ROM.
@endnode
